{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"A\":\"Build a low-risk full variant: parse fragments once, form each Cartesian candidate as L*10000000+R, sieve primes through 24514 once, and trial-divide only by those primes using integers. Preserve every prime occurrence, including duplicates, then numeric-sort and print one line per result.\",\"B\":\"Build an independent full variant using exact deterministic Miller-Rabin bases 2, 7, and 61; the certified cap is below its 4,759,123,141 bound. Use uint64_t modular products, handle small values correctly, enumerate all P*P pairs, and preserve multiplicity.\",\"C\":\"Target duplicate-fragment redundancy: run-length encode numeric left and right fragments, test each unique Cartesian cell once with B's proven Miller-Rabin kernel, and append a prime result countLeft*countRight times. Fixed seven-digit suffixes make this grouping injective; benchmark separately because grouping overhead can lose when all fragments differ.\",\"G\":\"Validate A, B, C, and any proposed hybrid as complete programs under the fixed contract and exact-byte output requirement. Select only the fastest valid measured program; do not assume merged optimizations add. Retain Cartesian-pair semantics, duplicate output occurrences, ascending order, and avoid benchmark-specific mappings, whole-range lookup tables, or new threading/build dependencies.\",\"analysis\":\"The sole measurement is a valid 0.0446933 s baseline (score 0.5). Its likely hot path is floating fmod over every integer divisor for up to 900 candidates; sqrt(600971707) floors to 24514. Arithmetic concatenation is exact because every right fragment has width seven, but separately permuted groups require preserving all admissible pair results.\"}"}}
